  "solution": {
    "text": "Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are Young&#39;s moduli of the material of the rods and <math> <mrow> <msub> <mi>&#945;</mi> <mn>1</mn> </msub> <mtext>&#160;and&#160;</mtext> <msub> <mi>&#945;</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math> are the coefficients of thermal expansion. Using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>stress</mi><mi>strain</mi></mfrac></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>&#945;</mi><mi>t</mi></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></math>is the temperature. Tension must be the same in both the rods for their junction to be in equilibrium.<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#945;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn mathvariant=\"italic\">2</mn></msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#945;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>t</mi></math>"
  }
